[center][size=6]MO2 Modlist Vault[/size]
[size=3]A Wabbajack-style backup & restore tool for your Mod Organizer 2 setup - minimal backup size, zero manual FOMOD clicking on restore.[/size][/center]

[center][color=#f0a028][b]This is a standalone tool, not an MO2 plugin.[/b] It runs alongside MO2 and reads/writes an instance folder directly. An optional companion MO2 plugin ([b]MO2 Vault Integration[/b]) adds a one-click "Create Restore Point" button inside MO2 itself.[/color][/center]

[size=4][b]What this is[/b][/size]
Zipping up a whole modlist for backup means storing gigabytes of content you can just redownload from Nexus at any time. MO2 Modlist Vault instead works out, for every mod, exactly which files came from which archive in your downloads folder (matched by content hash, not filename), and stores that as a small [b]recipe[/b] instead of the files themselves. Only content with genuinely no redownload source - a hand-placed custom mod, a tool's generated output folder, a patched .exe - gets bundled raw, with no size cap.

Restoring replays the recipe: redownload the archive, extract it, drop the matched files straight into the mod folder. [b]No installer runs. No FOMOD dialog ever appears.[/b] MO2 itself only needs to be opened once afterward, to pick up plugin load order.

[size=4][b]Features[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Recipe-based reconstruction[/b] - SHA1 hash matching, not name/path guessing, so restores are exact and installer-free.
[*][b]Minimal size[/b] - only truly unbundlable content is stored raw, no cap.
[*][b]Captures your whole environment[/b] - MO2 itself + third-party plugins, the tools/ folder (xEdit, DynDOLOD, Synthesis, BodySlide output, etc., including multi-archive tools), profile settings and load order, mod/separator highlight colors, and an optional local "Stock Game" copy overlay (e.g. a Creation Kit Platform Extended patcher).
[*][b]Game-agnostic[/b] - tested on both Skyrim Special Edition and Fallout 4 modlists.
[*][b]Vault mode[/b] - take repeated, [i]deduplicated[/i] snapshots over time instead of one full backup per run (Time Machine-style). Unchanged content is stored once no matter how many snapshots reference it. Each snapshot shows a changelog against the last one and restores independently.
[*][b]GUI and CLI[/b] - a full desktop app with a persistent log panel and remembered settings, or script it entirely from the command line.
[*][b]Portable settings[/b] - your paths and Nexus API key live in a plain, deletable JSON file, not the registry - safe to hand a copy of this tool to someone else.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]A Nexus Premium API key (for redownloading during restore - not needed for backup or vault snapshotting).
[*]Your original portable MO2 release .7z, if you want MO2 itself and third-party plugins captured/restored automatically.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Download and extract the archive anywhere - it's a self-contained folder, no installer.
[*]Run [b]mo2-modlist-vault.exe[/b] to open the GUI.
[*]See the included Quick Start guide for a first backup/restore walkthrough.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Known limitations[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Restoring needs MO2 (and its helper processes) closed - it writes directly into the live instance folder. Use the built-in "Close MO2 Processes" button if needed.
[*]Vault snapshotting currently re-hashes your full modlist every time rather than skipping unchanged files - fine for a few hundred mods, slower on very large ones.
[/list]

